Z||000000||JetroGT||03-09-2001||08:08 PM||||I installed AC Delco R43TS plugs in my SE.<BR>The old plugs were R42TS plugs. They look the exactly the same except for the center insulator on the new plug looks thinner. Is it going to affect my heat range? I don't want to burn holes in my pistons. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||03-09-2001||09:05 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.acdelco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.acdelco.com</A>  I think they have an online catalog. they have email. they can tell you for sure what the diferance is.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JetroGT||03-10-2001||07:09 AM||||This is what I found<P>R= Resistor <BR>4= 14 mm Thread <BR>6= Heat Range <BR>T= Taper Seat <BR>S= Extended Tip  <P>So my heat range is different. Going from 2 to 3. So is the lower number colder or hotter?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by JetroGT (edited 03-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||pontiacs4me||03-10-2001||11:29 AM||||higher the heat range number the hotter the plug.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||03-11-2001||12:48 PM||||My Formula had R43TS's in it and I switched back to 42's. I haven't driven it enough to tell a difference yet.  If you drive hard, the 43's might overheat and burn a little, but no danger to the car.  It'll just wear out the plugs quicker.  Check them after a some hard driving and see if they look burnt or glazed.  Most shop manuas have color photos to compare yours to.  The 43's in mine looked slightly overheated, but not bad.  You should be fine with either plug.||||reg||1
